[[Image:Bluebells in Winkworth.jpg|thumb|right|Winkworth with Spring bluebells]]
'''Winkworth Arboretum''' is a [[National Trust]]-owned [[arboretum]] in the civil parish of [[Munstead and Tuesley]] between [[Godalming]] and [[Hascombe]], south-west [[Surrey]], [[England]].

The {{cvt|95|acres}} arboretum was founded by Dr [[Wilfrid Fox]], starting in 1938 and continuing through [[World War II]]. He cleared the land and planted it with carefully chosen trees and shrubs to maximise its autumnal appearance.<ref>{{cite web |title=The history of Winkworth Arboretum |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/winkworth-arboretum/features/the-history-of-winkworth-arboretum |website=Winkworth Arboretum |publisher=National Trust |access-date=21 December 2019}}</ref> Once it was established, he presented it to the National Trust in 1952.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Church |first1=Richard |title=The Shell Guide to England - The South East |date=1977 |publisher=Michael Joseph |location=London |page=186}}</ref>

Winkworth Arboretum exhibits over 1000 species of trees as well as large collections of [[azalea]], [[rhododendron]], and [[holly]] on slopes leading down to landscaped garden lakes.
