'''Gatton Park''' is a [[country estate]] set in parkland landscaped by [[Capability Brown]] and gardens by [[Henry Ernest Milner]] and [[Edward White (landscape architect)|Edward White]] at [[Gatton, Surrey|Gatton]], near [[Reigate]] in [[Surrey]], England.

Gatton Park is now partly owned by [[The Royal Alexandra and Albert School]] and partly by the [[National Trust]]. It comprises {{convert|500|acre|km2}} of [[demesne|manor]], half on the school site and half on National Trust land. The property is [[Listed building|Grade II listed]].<ref>{{NHLE|grade=II|desc=Gatton Hall|num=1377943|date=31 March 1977}}</ref> The park is Grade II listed on the [[Register of Historic Parks and Gardens of special historic interest in England|Register of Historic Parks and Gardens]].<ref name=NHLEpark>{{NHLE|num=1001409|desc=Lower Gatton Park |access-date=22 March 2018 |grade= II |fewer-links= yes }}</ref>

Most of the park and gardens are closed to the public but opened on specific regular open days. There is an open National Trust trail on the west side of the original estate.

==History==
[[File:Gatton Park - Dec 2011 - The Big House.jpg|thumb|Gatton Park - Dec 2011 - The Big House]]
{{main|Gatton, Surrey}}
The manor's history can be traced to the [[Domesday Book]] of 1086.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://opendomesday.org/place/TQ2752/gatton/ |title= Gatton |author= Powell-Smith A |year= 2011 |publisher= Open Domesday |access-date= 29 November 2023 }}</ref> In 1449, John Timperley, steward to [[Henry VI of England|Henry VI]], was given permission to [[enclosure|enclose]] the land and, two years later, the manor was granted the privilege of sending two members to Parliament, which it retained, as a "[[rotten borough]]", until the [[Reform Act 1832|Parliamentary reform of 1832]].<ref name=Booton_1992>{{cite news |last= Booton |first= Peter |date= October 1992 |title= 'Manor, Borough, Parish, Park': Something 'rotten' in the state of Gatton? |work= Surrey County Magazine |volume= 23 |issue= 10 |pages= 15–16 }}</ref>{{efn|For this aspect of Gatton, see [[Gatton (UK Parliament constituency)]].}}{{efn|John Timperley had voted in favour of [[Henry VI of England|Henry VI's]] marriage to [[Margaret of Anjou]].<ref name=Booton_1992/>}} During the reign of [[Henry VIII]], Gatton was the property of the Crown.<ref name=Booton_1992/>

The earliest known house is thought to have been constructed in the [[Tudor architecture|Tudor]] or [[Jacobean architecture|Jacobean]] periods. Although there is no surviving mention of the building in historical records, it was discovered during archaeological investigations in the mid-1930s. On the same site as the current house, the external dimensions were around {{cvt|90|ft|m|0|order=flip}} by {{cvt|24|ft|m|0|order=flip}}. Built of white sandstone, it had a symmetrical floor plan with two forward-projecting [[wing (building)|wings]]. Beneath the west wing was a small, brick-built chamber, which may had been used as a strong room or safe deposit for valuable items. The archaeologist, [[S. E. Winbolt]], who investigated the site, suggested that this early house might have been constructed during a time of religious persecution.<ref>{{cite journal |last= Winbolt |first= S. E. |year= 1934 |title= Discoveries at Gatton Park House |journal= Surrey Archaeological Collections |volume= 42 |pages= 121–122 |url= https://archaeologydataservice.ac.uk/catalogue/adsdata/arch-379-1/dissemination/pdf/vol_42/surreyac042_109-122_notes.pdf |access-date= 6 December 2023 }}</ref>

In the 17th century, the house is mentioned as being in the possession of John Weston of [[Sutton Place, Surrey]], the second and eldest surviving son of [[Richard Weston (1591-1652)|Sir Richard III Weston]]) and his wife, Mary Copley (daughter and heiress of William Copley of Gatton) until 1654.{{sfn|Harrison|1899|pp=116, 134, 137}}

Following the death of the previous owner, William Newland, in 1749, [[James Colebrooke]] acquired Gatton Park in 1751. He died ten years later and the estate passed to his brother, [[Sir George Colebrooke, 2nd Baronet|Sir George Colebrooke]], who employed [[Capability Brown]] to landscape the estate between 1762 and 1768.{{sfn|Manning|Bray|1974|p=232}}<ref name=SSD>{{cite web |title= Gatton Park |date= 21 August 2023 |publisher= Stiftung Schloss Dyck |url= https://www.eghn.org/en/gatton-park-2/ |access-date= 18 December 2023 }}</ref><ref name="times">{{cite newspaper The Times |last= Binney |first= Marcus |date= 2 August 2004 |title= Capability Brown park returns to life  |issue= 68143 |page= 27 |url= https://www.thetimes.com/travel/destinations/europe-travel/capability-brown-park-returns-to-life-vbrqzt6h7nd |access-date= 29 November 2023 }}</ref> To create the lakes, Colebrooke appropriated around {{cvt|40|acre|ha}} of [[glebe]] land and damned the tributaries of the [[River Mole]] that ran across it.<ref name=Hunt_1989>{{cite news |last= Hunt |first= Roger |date= March 1989 |title= A rascally spot of earth |work= Surrey County Magazine |volume= 20 |issue= 3 |pages= 30–32 }}</ref>

In 1789 Thomas Kingscote went to live at Gatton Park after his friend, Robert Ladbroke, had bought it in the same year. It was a [[Gatton (UK Parliament constituency)|notorious pocket borough]] and Thomas went there in order to manage the election of Ladbroke's nominees. Ladbroke bought it from the Graham family. In 1809, Richard Maliphant carried out repairs of the house for Henry Harpur-Crewe, who was renting the property at the time.{{sfn|Colvin|2008|p=673}}

In 1830, Gatton was purchased by [[Baron Monson|Frederick John Monson, 5th Baron Monson]] (1809–1841), for £100,000,<ref name=Booton_1992/><ref name=SSD/> for the ancient privilege of sending two members to the House of Commons, a perquisite that was cancelled two years later, "and all Lord Monson had for £100,000 was the land".{{sfn|Parker|1921|p=352}} He set about remaking Gatton Hall splendid: for him [[Thomas Hopper (architect)|Thomas Hopper]] made alterations to Gatton, but further plans were not executed.{{sfn|Colvin|2008|p=543}} The marble hall at the centre of the main block was [[revet]]ted in marble, even to the inlaid marbles of its floor, taking as a general model the Corsini Chapel in [[Basilica of San Giovanni in Laterano|San Giovanni in Laterano]],<ref>{{cite news |last= Cracknell |first= Basil |date= July 1979 |title= Famous Surrey families : The Colmans of Gatton Park |work= Surrey County Magazine |volume= 10 |issue= 3 |pages= 28–29, 51 }}</ref> though Lord Monson did not cap his hall with a dome. The walls were frescoed by [[Joseph Severn]] with the Four Classical Virtues, embodied by historical ladies.{{sfn|Parker|1921|p=352}}

In 1841 the estate was inherited by the 6th Baron Monson who lived in Lincolnshire and who let Gatton, first to his aunt and then to [[Hugh Cairns, 1st Earl Cairns]], the [[Attorney General]].<ref>{{cite web|url =http://www.burton-by-lincoln.info/burton%20folk%201861.pdf|title=The Life and Times of Burton Folk|access-date=13 November 2014 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141218112603/http://www.burton-by-lincoln.info/burton%20folk%201861.pdf |archive-date= 18 December 2014 |url-status= usurped }}</ref>

Between 1888 and 1942, Gatton Park was owned by [[Sir Jeremiah Colman, 1st Baronet|Sir Jeremiah Colman]] whose family had established the [[Colman's|Colman's mustard]] food brand in the early 19th century.<ref>{{cite news |title= Gatton Park open day |date= 27 February 1997 |work= Surrey Mirror |page= 6}}</ref> In February 1934, a fire destroyed much of the house.<ref>{{cite news |title= Disastrous fire at Gatton Hall |date= 9 February 1934 |work= Surrey Mirror and County Post |issue= 2923 |page= 5 }}</ref> The house was rebuilt under the supervision of [[Edwin Cooper (architect)|Edwin Cooper]], using stone reclaimed from the destroyed building. The main portico, bronze gates and a statue entitled ''The Athlete in Repose'' had survived intact and were incorporated into the new structure.<ref>{{cite news |title= Mansion rises from ashes |date= 16 February 1935 |work= Daily Telegraph |issue= 24877 |page= 17 }}</ref> Construction work was completed in 1936.<ref name=GattonFoundation>{{cite web |title= Gatton Park & Gatton Hall |publisher= Royal Alexandra & Albert School Foundation |url= http://www.gattonfoundation.org.uk/gatton-park-hall.asp |access-date= 7 December 2023 }}</ref> Colman died in January 1942.<ref>{{cite newspaper The Times |title= Sir Jeremiah Colman |date= 17 January 1942 |issue= 49136 |page= 2 }}</ref>

The property was requisitioned during the [[Second World War]] for the [[Canadian Army]] and the [[Royal Military Police]].<ref name=GattonFoundation/> Part of the estate was purchased in 1948 for the [[Royal Alexandra and Albert School]],<ref>{{cite news |title= Gatton Park sold |date= 13 May 1948 |work= Daily Telegraph |issue= 28980 |page= 5 }}</ref> which is now a state boarding school.<ref>{{cite news|title=Lessons from a state-funded boarding school|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/education/7992093/Lessons-from-a-state-funded-boarding-school.html|publisher=[[Daily Telegraph]]|date=13 September 2010}}</ref> 108 acres of woodland was given to the National Trust in 1951 by Colman's son, also called Jeremiah Colman.<ref>{{cite newspaper The Times |title= Sir Jeremiah Colman |date= 10 January 1961 |issue= 54975 |page= 15 }}</ref>

==St Andrew's Church==
[[File:St Andrew's Church, Gatton.jpg|thumb|right|St Andrew's Church, Gatton showing grave of Jeremiah Coleman (large headstone far right)]]
Near the hall stands the 13th-century [[St Andrew's Church, Gatton|St Andrew's Church]], a Grade I listed building.<ref>{{NHLE|num=1294726|desc= Church of St Andrew |access-date= 11 November 2014 |grade= I |fewer-links= yes }}</ref> The church, essentially a chapel for the estate that is reached from the house by a covered walkway, was richly improved within its simple exterior with imported woodwork in 1834: the pulpit and altar, bought from [[Nuremberg]], were optimistically attributed at the time to [[Albrecht Dürer]]; the carved doors came from [[Rouen]]; the presbytery stalls from a disestablished monastery in [[Ghent]],{{efn|When a party from the Surrey Archaeological Society visited in 1850, "Gatton church, as restored by the late Lord Monson, was much admired. The fittings of the interior were mostly purchased by his lordship in Belgium, during the confusion of the [[Belgian Revolution|revolution of 1830]], and thus rescued from further desecration."<ref name=GM>{{cite magazine |title= Surrey Archaeological Society |date= August 1860 |magazine= The Gentleman's Magazine |volume= 9 |page= 154 |url= https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=pst.000068790950&seq=166 |access-date= 18 December 2023 }}</ref>}} the altar rails came from [[Tongeren]];<ref name=Booton_1992/> stained glass for the windows, and the wainscoting of the nave and carved canopies came from [[Aarschot]], near [[Leuven]].<ref name=Hunt_1989/>

The Gothic screen at the west end came from an unidentified English church, where it had been dismantled and was about to be burnt.<ref name=GM/> "Gatton, rebuilt in the 1830s, is a ''bijou''" reported [[Nikolaus Pevsner]] "perhaps the best example in the country of the tendency for the church to become an extension of the landlord's parlour or sculpture gallery."{{sfn|Nairn|Pevsner|Cherry|1971|p=60}} In 1930, stones from the structure were removed by Sir Jeremiah Colman and the contemporary rector of Gatton and given to [[Colorado College]] of [[Colorado Springs, Colorado]] in the United States to be incorporated into the structure of the [[Shove Memorial Chapel|Eugene Percy Shove Memorial Chapel]] in honour of the donor's ancestor, Edward Shove, who was rector of Gatton from 1615 to 1646.{{sfn|Fuller|Seay|1981|p=8}}

==Other features of the park==
[[File:Gatton Town Hall.JPG|right|thumb|Gatton Town Hall]]
Gatton Town Hall is a [[Grade II* listed]] ornamental garden temple.<ref>{{NHLE|num=1029114|desc= Gatton Town Hall |grade= II* |access-date=11 November 2014 |fewer-links= yes }}</ref> Attributed to the architect, [[Robert Taylor (architect)|Robert Taylor]], it consists of a pedimented roof, supported by six [[cast iron]] [[Doric order|Doric]] columns.<ref name=Booton_1992/> It is the place where, prior to 1832, the tiny electorate of the Gatton rotten borough voted in their two members of Parliament. Behind the structure is a stone urn, carved with serpents entwined, on a deep moulded [[pedestal|plinth]] inscribed "in memory of the deceased Borough".{{sfn|O'Brien|Nairn|Cherry|2022|p=354-355}}

During the 1860s, Colman commissioned [[Henry Ernest Milner]] to design the [[parterre]], Pleasure Gardens and Old World Garden.<ref name=Gatton>{{cite web |title=The Park & Gardens, the Parterre |url=http://www.gattonpark.com/parkandgardens.html |publisher=Gatton Trust |access-date=11 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150411191909/http://www.gattonpark.com/parkandgardens.html |archive-date=11 April 2015 |url-status=live |mode=cs2 |df=dmy }}</ref><ref name=ODNB>{{cite ODNB |last=Elliott |first=Brent |title=Milner, Henry Ernest (1845–1906) |edition=online |year=2004 |url=http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/article/37771 |access-date=9 April 2015 |doi=10.1093/ref:odnb/37771 |mode=cs2}}</ref> Milner's son in law  [[Edward White (landscape architect)|Edward White]] created the Rock Garden with [[James Pulham and Son]]<ref>{{cite web | title=The Pulham Legacy 1910-1912 -Gatton Park, Reigate|url=https://pulham.org.uk/2012/06/01/13-june-2012-gatton-park | access-date=22 February 2024}}</ref> and the Japanese garden for Colman. Both gardens were restored in the late-1990s.<ref>{{cite news |title= A taste of Japan revealed in the undergrowth |date= 28 October 1999 |work= Surrey Mirror |page= 13 }}</ref>

The school dormitories, to the north of the house, were designed by Adams, Gray and Adamson in 1954. The same firm designed the school chapel two years later.{{sfn|O'Brien|Nairn|Cherry|2022|p=354-355}}

[[File:The Millennium Stones, Gatton Park - geograph.org.uk - 1319809.jpg|thumb|right|The Millennium Stones in Gatton Park.]]
In the park, and accessible from the public footpath, is a stone circle called ''The Millennium Stones'' created by the sculptor [[Richard Kindersley]] to mark the double millennium in 2000. It is made from flat Caithness flagstones quarried in the far north of Scotland near [[Thurso]]. The first stone in the series is inscribed with the words from [[St John's Gospel]], "in the beginning the word was". The other nine stones are carved with quotations contemporary with each 200 year segment of the 2000 year period, ending with the words of [[T.S. Eliot]]: "At the still point of the turning world. Neither flesh nor fleshless; Neither from nor towards; at the still point, there the dance is, But neither arrest nor movement."<ref>[http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=25005  Gatton Park, The Millennium Stones ] The Megalithic Portal, 9 February 2010.</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Lovell |first= Cara |date= 5 April 2004 |title= Gatton enters stone age |work= Redhill, Reigate, Horley Life |url= https://www.redhillandreigatelife.co.uk/news/477359.gatton-enters-stone-age/ |access-date= 6 December 2023 }}</ref>

